committer hackerman-kl <alexandremalki89@gmail.com> 1788418175 +0200

The mutation arms bound their waits in DUT cycles, not host time, because both harnesses already do

The arms landed with a host deadline around each mutant run, which was
belt-and-braces for a hang that neither harness can actually produce. Both
bound their own waits in DUT CYCLES: the gasket harness does it so that a
wedged gasket FAILS the suite instead of hanging it - the very property
the first gasket mutant exercises - and tsngen puts an explicit tick
ceiling on every wait it performs.

So the deadline answered a question the DUT already answers, and answered
it in a machine-dependent way: the same mutant could be a catch on one
host and a TIMEOUT on a slower one. A mutation arm whose verdict depends
on how fast the machine is cannot be evidence about the assertions.

The runs are now plain subprocess calls, the TIMEOUT branch and the
process-group kill machinery are gone with the deadline that needed them,
and the reason is recorded where the verdict is read. Both arms still
pass unchanged: the gasket arm at 5/5 and tsngen at 4/4, every mutant
caught and both positive controls passing.

